        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Chor Voli',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Chor Voli is a traditional Afghan partition card game where players divide their 13-card hands into up to four 3-card combinations, similar to Brag hands. These combinations are then played out in a series of showdowns starting with each player's strongest hand. The game is related to the British game Crash and the Pakistani variant Ramchi.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Afghanistan\nTime Period: 20th century\nHistorical Development: Learned by Gyula Zsigri from Sherzod Lukmoni in 1992. A similar game called Ramchi was described in 2011 by Tilman Schieber, said to have come from Pakistan where it is popular with Afghan refugees.\nCultural Significance: Played in Afghanistan and by Afghan refugee communities in Pakistan.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 4\nMaximum Players: 4\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: No, individual play',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card international pack\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nChips or counters for scoring\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: 13 cards each\nDealing Pattern: Counterclockwise\nDealer: Left-hand opponent cuts, dealer can deal in any convenient packets',
              },
            ],
          },
